<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Farrukhsiyar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1685">1685</a>–<a href="/years/1719">1719</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Farrukhsiyar was a Mughal emperor who ruled from 1713 to 1719, a son of Azim-ush-Shan and grandson of Bahadur Shah I. He won the throne with the help of the Sayyid brothers, Abdullah Khan and Husain Ali Khan, who then dominated his government. In 1717 he granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> a farman exempting its trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from customs duties, and in 1719 the Sayyids deposed, blinded and killed him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/farrukhsiyar.jpg" alt="Farrukhsiyar" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gurkan1526 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhhamad_Farukhsiyar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Farrukhsiyar was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a> to a Kashmiri mother, Sahiba Niswan, the second son of Azim-ush-Shan and a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>. He spent his early years governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>, the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. In 1712 Azim-ush-Shan was defeated by an alliance that placed Farrukhsiyar's uncle Jahandar Shah on the throne. The prince considered suicide but was dissuaded by his friends from Bengal, and he sought revenge for his father's death, joined by Husain Ali Khan, the governor of Bengal, and his brother Abdullah Khan, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 10 January 1713 Farrukhsiyar's forces met Jahandar Shah's at Samugarh, 14 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>. Jahandar Shah was defeated and imprisoned, and Farrukhsiyar proclaimed himself emperor the next day. Abdullah Khan demanded the post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">wazir</a> and Farrukhsiyar finally gave in, while his brother Husain Ali Khan became commander-in-chief. Because the Sayyid brothers controlled the strongest part of the army, they became the real rulers of the empire, and Farrukhsiyar was emperor in little more than name.</p><p>His government fought several campaigns. In 1714 Husain Ali Khan forced Ajit Singh of Marwar to submit, and Ajit Singh gave a daughter in marriage to the emperor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-singh-ii">Jai Singh II</a> besieged the Jat leader Churaman at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thun">Thun</a>, and Churaman accepted Mughal authority in 1718. After a siege at Gurdaspur the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-singh-bahadur">Banda Singh Bahadur</a> surrendered, and on 19 June 1716 he and his followers were executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. In 1717 Farrukhsiyar issued a farman allowing the British East India Company to trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> duty-free, which its officials misused for private trade and which angered the nawab of Bengal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Farrukhsiyar's advisers Mir Jumla III and Khan Dauran told him that the Sayyids were unfit for office, and the two sides struggled for years. In February 1719 Husain Ali Khan entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> with a large army, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> horsemen led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaji-vishwanath">Balaji Vishwanath</a>, and Abdullah Khan seized the palace. Farrukhsiyar was captured, blinded and eventually killed in prison, and the Sayyids crowned Prince Rafi-ud-darjat in his place. He was known afterwards as Shahid-i-marhum, the martyr received with mercy. The town of Farrukhnagar near Delhi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhabad">Farrukhabad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Farrukhsiyar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muhammad Khan Bangash, an Afghan commander who served the Mughal emperor <b>Farrukhsiyar</b>, was granted lands in the Doab and founded the town of Farrukhabad in 1714, naming it after the emperor.</blockquote><a href="/state/bangash-nawabs-of-farrukhabad">Bangash Nawabs of Farrukhabad</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.88,75.32&amp;year=1707" aria-label="Aurangabad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6582 1427 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Farrukhsiyar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7032 1697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7032 1697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.88,75.32&amp;year=1707">What was here in 1707? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
